## Commands

| Task          | Command          |
| ------------- | ---------------- |
| Install       | `pnpm install`   |
| Run (dev)     | `pnpm dev`       |
| Lint          | `pnpm lint`      |
| Type check    | `pnpm typecheck` |
| Build         | `pnpm build`     |
| Run the build | `pnpm start`     |
| Write the migrations | `pnpm db:generate` |
| Apply the migrations | `pnpm db:migrate`  |

## Database

The app uses Postgres with Drizzle ORM. Copy `.env.example` to
`.env.local`, and set `DATABASE_URL`. The schema is in
`src/shared/db/schema/`. `pnpm db:generate` writes the SQL migrations
into `drizzle/`: keep them in version control. With
`DATABASE_MIGRATE=true`, the server applies them when it starts.

## Docker

`docker compose up --build` starts Postgres and the app on
<http://localhost:3000>. The app applies the migrations when it starts.
The `Dockerfile` builds a standalone Next.js server that runs as a
non-root user. If `drizzle/` does not exist, the build writes the first
migration from the schema.

## Structure

The code follows [Feature-Sliced Design](https://feature-sliced.design).
The Next.js `app/` folder holds only the routes. Each route renders a
page from the FSD `pages` layer. The FSD layers are in `src/`. A layer
imports only the layers below it:

| Layer | Content |
| ----- | ------- |
{% for layer, text in nextjs.layers %}
| `src/{{ layer }}` | {{ text }} |
{% endfor %}

The empty `pages/` folder in the root stops Next.js from reading
`src/pages` as routes of the Pages Router.

## Traces

Comments such as `// @trace pages.projects` refer to the elements of
the app definition. `appgen check` reads them and reports the elements
without code and the traces that are not valid.
